    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "Physical pair zero exclusion and its allocation polynomial infrastructure.", H("Physical pair zero exclusion and its allocation polynomial infrastructure."),
        Blocks(Paragraph(Text("For a finite vertex type, write kappa(d)=d^2(d^2-1)/24, c_d=(d^2-3)/(4(d-1)) when d differs from one and c_1=0. Put L_1=13/48 and, for d at least two, L_d=d(d^2-1)(d-3)/96+d(d+1)(2d-3)/(16(d-1)). The form H is one half sum_v d_v^2 t_v^2 plus one half sum_uv a_uv t_u t_v minus z/4 times sum_v d_v(d_v^2-1)t_v plus z^2 sum_v kappa(d_v). The form Q is the pairQuadratic of the source, with one half the ordered distinct-vertex sum of d_u d_v(d_u d_v-a_uv)(z+eta_u)(z+eta_v)/9 and diagonal term kappa(d_v)(z^2+2z eta_v). The Cayley map is c(w)=(1-w)/(1+w), and eta(d,X)_v=c((-1)^d_v X^d_v).")), Paragraph(Text("The complexification is bilinear, with no conjugates. The graph identity retains the leaf constraint t_v=2z/3 and the exact 13/48 compensation per leaf. The internal degree-2 remainder is 5/16. The finite index types have no cardinality bound. The real parts of the physical Cayley variables are positive. Leaves obey eta_v=z in real and imaginary parts. The positive real quadratic value, the real sum-of-squares certificate and the bilinear lemma imply nonvanishing for every complex X of norm less than one; independent arbitrary leaf weights are outside this statement.")),
