Paragraph(Text("Write F(d,r;X)=1+(-1)^d X^(d+r) and P(X)=sum_alpha w_alpha product_v F(d_v,r_alpha,v;X). Let h count the vertices of odd d_v. Define the quotient vertex factor as the geometric sum from i=0 to d+r-1 when d is odd, and as F(d,r;X) when d is even; Q is the same weighted allocation sum with these quotient factors. The notation p_C means extension of real coefficients to the complex numbers, ord_1 means root multiplicity at one, and H_N(p)(u,v)=sum over k at most N of p_k u^k v^(N-k) is canonical homogenization. Natural degree uses zero as the natural degree of the zero polynomial.")), Paragraph(Text("The hypotheses quantify over arbitrary finite allocation types, positive degrees and weights, a common total mass and even degree sum. No coefficient cancellation is assumed. Every odd-degree vertex contributes one factor 1-X; the quotient has strictly positive value at one. Reciprocity transfers open-disk exclusion to the exterior. Homogenization uses the canonical Mathlib polynomial construction.")),
